@AnkushinDaniil yes, I wanted to make a similar point. To address this, I updated the formula to use the form `tx_gas_limit - tx_unused_gas` when computing the quotient.

--

After thinking about it more, I don't love that the receipt `gas_used` doesn't match the refund adjusted amount and/or that the sum of `gas_used` fields in the receipt does not equal the actual `gas_used` by the transaction. I think this is something we should think more about, but I'm okay with this for now while we continue to evaluate options. I think the refund mechanism is now sufficiently defined.

@Helkomine wrote:

> Although this is unrelated, I think we can abstract away the impact of EIP-2780. EIP-8037 only defines the state creation fee, EIP-8038 only defines the state access fee, and EIP-2780 uses these definitions in its intrinsic fee accounting, which allows for the elimination of circular dependencies between different EIPs, something we want to avoid.

This is a good point, will CC this to @misilva73. I believe the spec should end up, or actually be specified *now* without circular dependencies but indeed the structure where each EIP defines specific behavior or inherits this behavior from dependent EIPs.

Exciting EIP! I cannot approve this as editor though:

The Specification section currently defers everything to leanSpec, including this EIP's own parameters (e.g. SUBNET_COMMITTEES is used but never defined). Deferring the large shared spec to an external repo is fine and well-precedented: EIP-3675 (the Merge) does exactly this with ethereum/consensus-specs. But EIP-3675 still specifies its own changes inline, with concrete constants (`TERMINAL_TOTAL_DIFFICULTY = xxx`) and normative rules. Please mirror that split: keep leanSpec external, but specify the aggregator role's own parameters/rules inline with real values, or specify them as `TBD` if the values are not yet known. 

@g11tech also requested to remove the lean naming, this is only partly done, `leanSpec`, `leanVM`, `leanSig` are still being used. (@g11tech assuming you also want to get rid of the `lean` naming?)

